WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:05.560
"The Batman" takes place in a brand new version
of Gotham City which is completely different

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:11.690
to the one seen in the DC Extended Universe
features "Batman v Superman," "Justice League,"

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:13.130
and "Birds of Prey."

00:00:13.130 --> 00:00:18.370
Matt Reeves' vision for the urban jungle is
very much a grimy, dark place where it always

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.840
seems to be raining.

00:00:19.840 --> 00:00:24.520
The setting matches the equally moody new
version of Batman, who protects the innocent

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:28.390
from the hordes of criminals who move through
all corners of the city.

00:00:28.390 --> 00:00:33.550
When The Riddler starts his war on corruption
within the ranks of Gotham's political, financial,

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:38.930
and law enforcement spheres, Bruce Wayne has
to dive head-first into a sprawling mystery

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:43.780
that stretches from the mayor's office to
the bowels of the seedy Iceberg Lounge.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.829
The Riddler's brutal game reveals some devastating
truths about the city, and it even forces

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:52.000
Bruce to grapple with his family's legacy.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.250
Thomas and Martha Wayne’s memories were
tarnished both in the eyes of the public and

00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:01.239
Bruce by the time the credits roll, mainly
due to The Riddler revealing Thomas Wayne

00:01:01.239 --> 00:01:05.899
tried to keep the lid on Martha’s mental
illness treatment at Arkham Asylum which ultimately

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:09.039
spiraled out to involving Carmine Falcone
(KAR-mine fal-KOH-nee].”

00:01:09.039 --> 00:01:11.859
.
None of this matters to the citizens of Gotham,

00:01:11.859 --> 00:01:17.219
who must fight for survival when The Riddler
bombs the sea wall, flooding most of the city.

00:01:17.219 --> 00:01:22.020
To make matters worse, the Caped Crusader
has to battle a number of the villain's faithful

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:27.619
followers inside the roof of Gotham Arena
as they try to shoot crowds of survivors.

00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:32.860
It's a dramatic final battle that sees Batman
fighting The Riddler's violent ideology, as

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.590
embodied by his followers, and the fight nearly
gets the better of him.

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:40.880
But, thankfully, the Bat has a surprising
weapon in his utility belt.

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:45.759
During the fight on the arena's railings,
things go from bad to worse for the Bat when

00:01:45.759 --> 00:01:50.299
he ends up on his back after taking numerous
shots from Riddler's followers.

00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:55.919
Unfortunately, one of the shooters takes the
opportunity to attack Selina Kyle, who tries

00:01:55.919 --> 00:01:59.149
to stop the gang from committing mass murder.

00:01:59.149 --> 00:02:03.529
For a moment, it looks like one of them might
kill her, which causes Bruce to pull a small

00:02:03.529 --> 00:02:07.289
vial of green liquid out of his utility belt
and inject it.

00:02:07.289 --> 00:02:12.650
This green liquid gives Batman a much-needed
adrenaline boost, energizing him to the degree

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:16.590
that he can save Selina and finish the battle
for Gotham's soul.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:21.400
But that mysterious liquid is never actually
addressed, leaving us to wonder what exactly

00:02:21.400 --> 00:02:22.850
the Bat injected.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:28.330
It's hard not to see it as a nod towards the
classic villain, Bane. DC fans will know that

00:02:28.330 --> 00:02:33.050
the muscled behemoth uses a green formula
called "Venom" to give him enhanced strength

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:34.880
and speed in a fight.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:39.620
It typically gives him a brutal edge over
his opponents, not unlike the burst of ferocious

00:02:39.620 --> 00:02:44.180
energy Bruce gets in the final showdown near
the end of "The Batman."

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:48.600
This leaves audiences with a few questions
that we annoyingly don't get the answers to,

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.600
including: Has Batman already crossed paths
with Bane?

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:55.810
Or is he out there in the world waiting for
his chance to strike?

00:02:55.810 --> 00:03:00.720
Will Bane show up in a sequel to "The Batman,"
should that next movie ever be greenlit?

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.720
Probably not.

00:03:01.720 --> 00:03:06.900
The green liquid Bruce Wayne uses in the final
fight is not a big enough Easter egg to suggest

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:11.020
the man who broke the Bat is coming to Gotham
anytime soon.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:15.900
Plus, Matt Reeves has already opened up about
what he'd like to do with a second film if

00:03:15.900 --> 00:03:19.970
Warner Bros. gives him the go-ahead -- and
it doesn't sound like the hulking figure is

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.320
involved in the director's vision.

00:03:22.320 --> 00:03:27.360
In a recent interview with Collider, Reeves
explained that, when it comes to DC characters,

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.630
he wants to find the most "grounded" way of
telling their stories.

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:36.420
He went on to say he already has an idea of
how to adapt one of the most iconic villains

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:39.690
in Batman's rogue's gallery: Mr. Freeze.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:45.710
Plus, after The Riddler is safely in custody,
he speaks with his next-door cellmate, and

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:51.460
while he never mentions a name, it's fairly
obvious this character, played by Barry Keoghan,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.200
is supposed to be the newest version of the
Joker.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:04:00.210
So, while this is great news for fans wanting
more from Reeves' contributions to the DCEU,

00:04:00.210 --> 00:04:04.800
don't expect to see Bane go up against Robert
Pattinson's version of the Caped Crusader

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:11.900
any time soon.

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:16.090
With the director's "grounded" approach to
comic book storytelling, it probably wouldn't

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:21.699
be a good idea to use Bane anyway, since Christopher
Nolan already delivered a realistic version

00:04:21.699 --> 00:04:26.439
of the villain played by Tom Hardy in 2012's
"The Dark Knight Rises."

